{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a soft pink snow dusting the cobblestone paths of Philosopher’s Path. No guidebook could prepare me for the quiet reverence of Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion, shimmering above its mirror pond. I sat on a bench, sipping matcha from a ceramic cup, watching a monk sweep leaves with slow, meditative strokes.\n\nTokyo was a different rhythm—electric, neon, alive. In Shibuya, I got lost in the scramble crossing, swept up in a sea of suits and sneakers. I ate ramen at a 10-seat stall in Shinjuku, where the chef nodded without speaking and poured broth so rich it tasted like comfort itself. At Akihabara, I bought a tiny robot keychain—because why not?\n\nIn Nikko, I wandered through ancient cedar forests to Toshogu Shrine, its gold leaf glinting under autumn sun. An elderly woman offered me an onsen ticket with a bow and a smile. I soaked in the steamy water, surrounded by mist and silence, feeling more at peace than I had in years.\n\nThe trains ran on time. Strangers bowed. No one yelled. Even the vending machines felt polite.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of senbei, a folded origami crane, and a heart fuller than I expected. Japan doesn’t shout—it whispers. And if you listen closely, it tells you how to slow down, how to savor, how to be still.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan. I remembered how to breathe.\n\n(Word count: 298)", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005376085755415261}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -5.8412379075889476e-06}, {"token": " Words", "